INT. BAKERY - PREDAWN

A narrow shop. Racks of proofing dough under cloth. A radio plays low, in Portuguese. Flour in the air like smoke.

PILAR, 30s, scores loaves with a razor in fast, even strokes. Apron, forearms white to the elbow.

JONAH, 30s, the delivery driver, leans on the counter with a crate under one arm, and has been leaning there too long.

PILAR
You're early.

JONAH
You're late.

PILAR
I've been here since two.

JONAH
That's what I mean.

She doesn't smile. She slides a tray into the oven, closes the door with her hip, and wipes her hands on her apron, and he pretends to read the crate label while she does.

PILAR
You bring the rye flour?

JONAH
Forty pounds. And the thing you asked for.

He reaches into his jacket and pulls out a small paper bag, slightly crushed. She takes it, opens it, looks inside. A single cherry.

PILAR
One?

JONAH
They only had one.

PILAR
It's July.

JONAH
That's when they stop having them.

She sets the cherry on the cutting board and looks at it as if it were a coin from a country she has never visited.

Jonah sets down the crate. Steps closer. She doesn't step away.

PILAR (CONT'D)
(together)
You shouldn't.

JONAH ^
(together)
I wanted to.

They both laugh, quietly. The radio fills the gap.

The back door opens. MR. DELACROIX-WU, 70, her landlord, in a tweed cap and a rain-spotted coat, steps in stamping his shoes. He takes in the two of them, the cherry, the flour.

MR. DELACROIX-WU
Pilar. The roof.

PILAR
What about the roof?

MR. DELACROIX-WU
It leaks onto the dough.

Pilar and Jonah look at the ceiling together. A drop forms. Falls. Lands on a loaf with a soft plop. Nobody moves for a moment. Jonah picks up the cherry and sets it gently on the damp loaf, like a crown.
